// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/tools/t27o/spice_convert.t27 -- t27o (Ouroboros), its SPICE front end (#10509, M2a of the
// analog-emulation epic #10486): reads an ngspice netlist, flattens its subcircuits and prints an
// inventory, or refuses with the card, its line and the reason. It sits beside rust_convert.t27 as the
// spec said it would (specs/tools/t27o/<language>_convert.t27) and is reached by file extension:
// `tri t27o deck.spice | deck.cir | deck.sp`. M2b (#10766) adds the other direction of the same reader: the
// constants of the M1 linear cell (specs/analog/linear.t27) folded from a deck (see "The cell", below).
// --
// The dialect is ngspice's (the IHP open PDK targets ngspice). What this reader does with a deck:
// - The first physical line is the TITLE and is never a card (ngspice does the same). It is printed
//   back, so a first card eaten as a title is visible.
// - `*` starts a comment line, `;` and ` $` end a line early, `+` continues the card before it (comment
//   and blank lines between do not break the continuation), case is ignored, a tab is a space.
// - SI suffixes: t g meg k m u n p f, with `m` = milli and `meg` = mega (`1m` is 1e-3, `1meg` is 1e6);
//   any other trailing letters are a unit, as ngspice reads them (`10pF`, `5V`; also `1farad` = 1e-15,
//   the well-known ngspice trap). `mil` is refused, not guessed. A number is kept exact as a decimal
//   mantissa and a power of ten (0.13u is 13e-8): no float is involved. At most 18 significant digits.
// - Cards read: R C L (two nodes, a value, `m=`; a C and an L also `ic=`), V I (DC, PULSE, SIN, PWL),
//   B (a voltage of time alone: `B1 in 0 V = u(time-425n)`, see "The B source"), E (a VCVS: two node pairs
//   and a gain), X (an instance of a subcircuit or of a PDK device), `.subckt` / `.ends` (nested
//   definitions are scoped to the definition that holds them), `.lib <file> <section>` and `.include
//   <file>` (recorded, never followed: no PDK file is read), `.options TEMP=` and the integration choice
//   `method=gear|trap|trapezoidal`, `maxord=<number>`, `trtol=<number>` (recorded: OPT_METHOD, OPT_MAXORD),
//   `.PARAM name=literal`, `.tran`, `.ic` / `.nodeset` (`v(node)=literal`), `.save` and `.global`
//   (recorded), `.end`.
// - PDK devices are recognised by the model name of an X card and counted, not evaluated (evaluation is
//   M3): sg13_lv_nmos and sg13_lv_pmos (4 nodes, w l ng m) and cap_cmim (2 nodes, w l m). A `.subckt`
//   of the same name in the deck wins over the table, as it does in ngspice. cap_cmim is beyond what
//   #10509 lists: the acceptance deck holds 64 of them, so refusing it would have refused the deck.
// - `.control` ... `.endc`: `tran` is read like a `.tran` card; let plot set unset write run print echo
//   wrdata setplot display destroy quit exit save hardcopy are post-processing, counted as ignored;
//   any other command is refused (an `ac` or `op` would be an analysis nobody here evaluates).
// - Refused with `line N: <reason>` and the card text, never approximated: a B source that is not a voltage
//   of time alone, any other
//   element letter (D Q J K M G F H S W T U O P Z Y), `.measure`, `.func`, `.model` and every other
//   directive not listed above, a `{expression}` or 'quoted expression', a value that is not a literal
//   number, an instance parameter other than m (w l ng for PDK devices; ic for C and L), subcircuit parameters, a node
//   count that differs from the subcircuit's ports, an undefined subcircuit, a subcircuit nested deeper
//   than MAX_DEPTH, a subcircuit that instantiates itself, a fractional `m` on an instance, a `.subckt`
//   without `.ends`. Every card is read; the refusal reported is the one on the EARLIEST line, so a
//   forward reference to a missing subcircuit never hides a malformed card above it.
// --
// The end of the deck (the rule, #10509): the reader reads the WHOLE file, and the deck ends at the LAST
// `.end` card. A `.end` with more cards after it is a section break, recorded and not a terminator; the
// cards after the last `.end` are ignored and counted. Why: in top.spice (uberWAVE's SAR ADC golden
// netlist, xschem output) the first `.end` is at line 296 and the two `.lib` lines, `.GLOBAL` and all
// the `.subckt` definitions come after it; ngspice produced SAR_top_tb_tran.raw from that very file, so it
// must have seen the definitions, and a reader that stopped at line 296 would reject every instance in
// it. A deck with one `.end` and definitions after it is therefore NOT read as a whole: its definitions
// are ignored and its instances come back as undefined subcircuits, named with their line. The ngspice
// source is not at hand here; the evidence for the rule is the golden .raw, not the source.
// --
// Flattening: every instance gets the hierarchical name ngspice gives a subcircuit's contents, the path of
// X instance names then the card name, lowercased and joined by dots: `x3.x1.xm1`. (ngspice's device-letter
// first spelling, `m.x3.x1.xm1`, is not reproduced.) Only leaf devices are listed; the X cards that open a
// level are counted. An `m=` on an X card multiplies everything below it; the inventory reports MOS and
// MIM devices weighted by the product of the m of the instance and of its ancestors. Node names are not
// resolved through the port maps: the inventory counts and names, and the cell below reads flat decks and
// refuses an instance (wiring a hierarchy is M4d, #10754).
// --
// The B source: `B1 n+ n- V = expression`. An expression built from numbers, `time`, u u2 uramp abs sgn sin cos
// exp sqrt, + - * / ^ ( ) and commas is a function of time alone, so the source is an independent voltage like a
// V source: it counts as a voltage source (`v=` of the inventory, `b=` of the sources line, leaf label `b`).
// ngspice's own decks for the fixed-step runs (specs/analog/decks) drive their circuit with one, because a B
// source declares no breakpoint and the step stays on the grid. Anything else in the expression (v( i( a node or
// branch name, @, a comparison, a ?:) or a B current source is refused: that source reads the circuit.
// --
// The caller passes the deck, a work buffer (at least 2 * deck.len + 8192 bytes: the cleaned cards, then
// the path scratch), an output buffer and a zeroed state vector: FIXED counters, CELL_SLOTS of cell workspace,
// the subcircuit table (MAXDEF rows), the stack of definitions the flattener is inside (MAX_DEPTH), then
// CARD_SLOTS slots a card, as many cards as fit; a deck that does not fit is refused, never cut. The result
// is the number of bytes the output needs; the buffer was too small when that is above out.len. `exit_code`
// is 0 for an accepted deck and 1 for a refused one.
// --
// The cell (M2b, #10766). `emit_cell` reads a deck as the linear cell of specs/analog/linear.t27 and prints
// its constants CELL_N, CELL_D and CELL_NV as t27, from the netlist alone: no per-circuit text. The cell is
// x' = x + rnd(D x + N u), D = M - I and N the backward Euler fold of (G + C/dt) x' = (C/dt) x + B u, every
// word a Q.24 sign-magnitude u32 (bit 31 is the sign). What the deck must be, and what the converter does:
// - FLAT R, C, L and ONE input: a V source, or a B source of time alone, from a node to ground (`0`); its
//   plus node is the cell's input node and the source is the column of B (its waveform does not matter: the
//   run supplies u). An I or E source and an X card (a subcircuit instance or a PDK device) are refused as
//   outside the cell; so is a second input, a floating source, `m=`, an initial condition other than 0 (the
//   cell starts at rest), a node called gnd (ngspice's reading of that name is not checked here; write 0), a
//   C or L on the input node, and a deck with no state or with more than NMAX = 5.
// - THE STEP. Exactly one `.tran` (or the control `tran`) with all four numbers `tstep tstop tstart tmax`;
//   dt is the fourth, tmax. The deck must say `.options method=gear maxord=1`, which ngspice runs as backward
//   Euler: that is the method the cell folds. Without it ngspice integrates by the trapezoidal rule, and the
//   words would describe another run: refused.
// - THE STATES, the ordering rule. The unknowns are the free nodes numbered 1, 2, ... in the order their names
//   first appear in the R, C and L cards of the deck (names compared without case, the input node and `0`
//   left out), then one per inductor, in card order, its current stored times ZS. The tables of linear.t27
//   number their nodes 1, 2, ... by hand, and the four decks of specs/analog/decks name theirs 1, 2, ... in the
//   order they first appear, so the words come out in the order linear.t27 pins. A deck that names its nodes in another
//   order gets its states in that order (the tests show the ladder listed from its far end), and the block
//   lists which node or inductor each state is, so the order is stated, never implicit.
// - ZS, the characteristic impedance that keeps the inductor rows near the voltages': sqrt(L1 / C1) with L1 the
//   first inductor and C1 the first capacitor of the deck (100 Ohm for linear.t27's RLC), and 1 when the deck
//   lacks either. A value of the deck is the f64 nearest to its decimal text (dec_f64: one multiplication or
//   one division of exact operands), which is the literal linear.t27 writes (1.0e-9 for 1n).
// - THE FOLD. cell_solve stamps the cards into the work matrix [A | C/dt | B] rule for rule as linear.t27's
//   stamp does (R: G, and G u on the right-hand side when an end is the input; C: C/dt into A and into the
//   middle block; L: a branch row with KCL +-i/ZS at its nodes), solves it by linear.t27's own `eliminate`
//   and `mn_of`, and rounds D = M - I and N with linear.t27's own `q_sm`, by `use`: a word can not differ from
//   the cell's by a rounding rule. The stamp is the one part written again here: linear.t27's `stamp` hands a
//   local array to a function that writes it, which t27c gen-rust lowers to `&mut &(w)`, and the Rust of this
//   spec is what `tri t27o` runs. pair_add and src_add take and return the matrix instead. The tests hold the
//   copy to the original: the four circuits' words are linear.t27's pinned constants, bit for bit.
// - REFUSED: a constant beyond +-127 or a matrix with no solution (a floating node) is a refusal, not a word.
// - NOT DONE: a hierarchy (M4d), a MOS or any nonlinear device (M3), a current source, more than one input, a
//   dt that is not tmax, an integration other than backward Euler.
// --
// Writing this spec, five t27c behaviours shaped it (each is a compiler limit, not a choice):
// - `use` splices by NAME: every word of the spec outside a `//` comment, string literals and `;` lines
//   included, that is also a declaration of an imported module is pulled in with everything that
//   declaration calls. A local called `bit`, a message saying "at line" or ".param" pulled all of
//   rust_convert.t27 into the generated Rust (11185 lines, 5150 once the comments moved to `//`). So the
//   header is `//` prose, nothing here is named like a rust_convert declaration, and the .param
//   directive is compared in two pieces (ci_cat).
// - A slice parameter that is only read becomes `impl AsRef<[usize]>` in the generated Rust, and the caller
//   then moves its `&mut [usize]` into it: the card and definition tables are therefore indexed in place,
//   not through accessor fns, and find_def writes a slot so the state vector stays `&mut`.
// - A string literal has \n and \t escapes but no \r; the tests build their CRLF deck at run time.
// - Since this spec imports analog::linear, the same splice also pulls linear.t27's `run` (whose `step` is
//   declared by rust_convert too) from the string "run" of the control commands, and rust_convert's `method`
//   from the option word, `at`, `bad` and `unknowns` from a message or a local. Those words are spelled in
//   two pieces or avoided (ci_cat, the ~ of deck_text, other locals); the test decks write the option word
//   with a ~ for the o. After adding a word, regenerate the Rust: a function nobody here calls, in
//   gen/rust/tools/t27o/spice_convert.rs, is a word that pulled it.
// - gen-rust lowers a local array passed to a function that writes it as `&mut &(w)`, which rustc refuses:
//   cell_solve keeps its matrix local and moves it through pair_add and src_add by value.
// ASCII only (L3)
// phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module T27oSpice;

use tools::t27o::rust_convert::put;
use tools::t27o::rust_convert::puts;
use tools::t27o::rust_convert::put_num;
use tools::t27o::rust_convert::put_text;
use tools::t27o::rust_convert::is_space;
use tools::t27o::rust_convert::is_digit;
use tools::t27o::rust_convert::is_alpha;
use tools::t27o::rust_convert::out_is;
use analog::linear::{eliminate, mn_of, q_sm, is_free, ix, fabs64, WIDE, NMAX, NODE_IN, NODE_GND, NEG, RC_N, RC_D, RC_NV, RLC_N, RLC_D, RLC_NV, LAD3_N, LAD3_D, LAD3_NV, LAD5_N, LAD5_D, LAD5_NV};

